diff --git a/docs/ATELIER_SANDBOX_PROVIDER.md b/docs/ATELIER_SANDBOX_PROVIDER.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..eecd2be0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/ATELIER_SANDBOX_PROVIDER.md
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+# Atelier Sandbox Provider
+
+[Atelier](https://github.com/frak-id/atelier) orchestrates isolated dev sandboxes (Kata Containers
+on Kubernetes, or Docker) behind a small REST API. With `SANDBOX_PROVIDER=atelier` the control plane
+calls that API directly; there is no shim service to deploy.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- An **Atelier server** reachable from the control plane.
+- An **Atelier API key** (`atl_…`) for the Atelier user sessions run as. Every session sandbox is
+  owned by that user.
+- The **`open-inspect` image** built in Atelier: its `open-inspect` seed layers this repository's
+  sandbox runtime on Atelier's `dev-base`, from the bundle
+  `python3 packages/sandbox-images/cli.py pack --provider atelier` produces (see
+  [`integrations/open-inspect`](https://github.com/frak-id/atelier/tree/main/integrations/open-inspect)
+  in the Atelier repository). `build --provider atelier` is refused here: Atelier builds it.
+- The **control plane URL** reachable from Atelier sandboxes, like every provider.
+
+## Configuration
+
+| Env var            | Terraform variable | Required | Notes                                                        |
+| ------------------ | ------------------ | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| `SANDBOX_PROVIDER` | `sandbox_provider` | yes      | `"atelier"`                                                  |
+| `ATELIER_API_URL`  | `atelier_api_url`  | yes      | Atelier server origin, e.g. `https://atelier.example.com`    |
+| `ATELIER_API_KEY`  | `atelier_api_key`  | yes      | Atelier API key, sent as a Bearer token. Sensitive.          |
+| `ATELIER_IMAGE`    | `atelier_image`    | no       | Atelier image sessions boot from. Defaults to `open-inspect` |
+
+Per-session `cpuCores` / `memoryMib` settings map to Atelier's `resources.vcpus` (rounded up) and
+`resources.memoryMb`; unset, sessions get 2 vCPUs and 4096 MiB. Atelier never expires a sandbox on
+its own, so the sandbox timeout setting does not apply.
+
+Like Daytona and E2B, the provider has no fleet-wide model key: add the keys for your models (e.g.
+`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) as global secrets in Settings > Secrets. See [Secrets Management](SECRETS.md).
+
+## Lifecycle
+
+| Open-Inspect             | Atelier                                                                             |
+| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| Spawn                    | `POST /v1/sandboxes` (`personalize: false`), then wait for its `sandbox-create` job |
+| Inactivity stop          | `POST /v1/sandboxes/:id/pause`: the disk is snapshotted, compute released           |
+| Next prompt after a stop | `POST /v1/sandboxes/:id/resume`: same disk, runtime restarted (no-op when running)  |
+| Destroy                  | `DELETE /v1/sandboxes/:id`                                                          |
+
+`personalize: false` keeps Atelier from applying its user's toolboxes, default harness and git
+credentials: git access goes through Open-Inspect's brokered credential helper, as on every
+provider. The session environment is set on the runtime process only, not sandbox-wide.
+
+The spawn waits for the create job for up to 200 seconds, just under the control plane's 240-second
+connect watchdog: the job succeeds when the runtime starts, which is when its bridge connects, so a
+failed boot reports Atelier's own error. The first boot on a node that has not pulled the image yet
+can exceed that; pre-pull the image on Atelier's nodes.
+
+## Limits
+
+- **No prebuilt repository or environment images**: sessions clone and run setup on every fresh
+  spawn (resumes keep the disk).
+- **No code-server, web terminal, VNC or tunnel URLs**: Atelier's in-pod agent holds `0.0.0.0:7681`
+  and forwards declared ports from `0.0.0.0`, which collides with services that bind the pod
+  interface themselves. Sessions that enable them log a warning and run without them.
+- The runtime runs as Atelier's unprivileged `dev` user, and only `/home/dev` survives a stop (the
+  image points `/workspace` into it).
